import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/ArticleHeader.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Does 1Password Even Have a Free Plan? What Bitwarden's Free Tier Actually Gives You"
  description="One of these has no permanent free plan at all. Here's what that actually means for your choice, and what Bitwarden's free tier genuinely includes."
>
  <ArticleHeader
    category="Software"
    title="Does 1Password Even Have a Free Plan? What Bitwarden's Free Tier Actually Gives You"
  />

  <p>
    A lot of "1Password vs Bitwarden" comparisons frame this as two free
    tiers going head to head. That's not actually the choice you're
    making — and it's worth clearing up before anything else.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> 1Password doesn't have a permanent free
    plan at all — only a 14-day trial. Bitwarden's free plan, by contrast,
    is genuinely free with no time limit. If cost is the deciding factor,
    this isn't really a close comparison.
  </p>

  <h2>What Bitwarden's free tier actually includes</h2>

  <p>
    Unlimited password storage and a built-in password generator, with no
    trial window or expiration. It covers the core job a password manager
    exists to do. What it doesn't include at the free tier: a built-in
    authenticator for two-factor codes and emergency access tools, both of
    which are reserved for Bitwarden's paid tier.
  </p>

  <h2>What 1Password actually costs once the trial ends</h2>

  <p>
    1Password's cheapest individual plan runs about $2.99/month billed
    correctly (some comparisons cite a lower list price that only applies
    at longer commitment terms — check the current price on 1Password's
    own site before assuming a specific number, since subscription pricing
    shifts). There's no way to keep using it for free past the 14-day
    trial window.
  </p>

  <h2>So why would anyone pay for 1Password at all?</h2>

  <p>
    Because "free" and "best for you" aren't the same question. 1Password
    is generally regarded as having a more polished interface and stronger
    built-in extras out of the box, which matters more if you're setting
    this up for family members or a small team who won't tolerate a
    clunky tool. If you're evaluating this purely as an individual on a
    budget, that polish isn't worth paying for over Bitwarden's free tier
    covering the same core function. If you're setting up password
    management for people who'll actively resist using it if it's not
    smooth, the paid polish can genuinely pay for itself in adoption
    alone.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest verdict</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Want free, no trial, no expiration:</strong> Bitwarden. It's not a downgrade for the core job — unlimited passwords, generator, sync.</li>
    <li><strong>Setting this up for non-technical family members or a team:</strong> 1Password's polish may be worth the subscription, specifically because a tool people won't fight to use doesn't get used.</li>
    <li><strong>Want 2FA codes and emergency access without paying:</strong> Neither free tier fully covers this — that's paid-tier territory on both platforms.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Don't let a comparison article frame this as two equal free options —
    it isn't one. Start from what you're actually being asked to pay
    before comparing features.
  </p>
</Layout>
